Specifically, the EXISTS and NOT EXISTS conditions are transformed into LEFT JOIN operations with aggregate functions on the column(s) being selected in the sub-queries.\n\nFor example, the original query has an EXISTS condition:\n```sql\nEXISTS (select * from store_sales, date_dim where c.c_customer_sk = ss_customer_sk and ss_sold_date_sk = d_date_sk and d_year = 2002 and d_moy between 6 and 6+2 and ss_list_price between 77 and 166)\n```\nThis is rewritten to:\n```sql\nLEFT JOIN (SELECT join_column, AGG_FUNCTION(column2) AS agg_result FROM table2 GROUP BY join_column) AS sub_query ON table1.join_column = sub_query.join_column WHERE column = sub_query.agg_result\n```\nSimilarly, the NOT EXISTS conditions are also transformed into LEFT JOIN operations with aggregate functions on the column(s) being selected in the sub-queries.\n\nOverall, the query rewrite rule applies to the given SQL query by transforming the scalar sub-queries within the WHERE clause into LEFT JOIN operations with aggregate functions.', 'time': 17.774179697036743}
